The == operator is a comparison operator that compares the references (i.e., memory addresses) of two objects. It returns true if both objects point to the same memory location, and false otherwise. This means that == is case-sensitive and does not handle null values or primitive types.

On the other hand, the equals() method is a static method provided by the String class that checks whether two strings are equal. It returns true if the two strings have the same length and characters at every index position match. The equals() method also handles null values and primitive types.

In general, == is better than equals() because it is more robust and can handle cases where the objects may not reference the same memory locations. However, it is still important to use equals() when dealing with large amounts of data or when you need to ensure that two objects refer to the same object instance.

Here's an example code snippet demonstrating the usage of == and equals():

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str1 = "Hello";
        String str2 = "Hello";

        System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // Output: true

        String str3 = new String("Hello");
        String str4 = new String("Hello");

        System.out.println(str3.equals(str4)); // Output: false
    }
}
```

In this example, we first create two strings and then print their equality using ==. We then create another string and check its equality again using equals(). Note that the second call to equals() will return false since they are different instances of the String class.
